    {
      "text": "S,K. This system is highly sensitive to",
      "bbox": [
        335.3499755859375,
        428.4506530761719,
        521.9320068359375,
        440.7461853027344
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "noise, which is a key reason we addressed it in this paper. For example, Figure 6 shows",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        441.9996337890625,
        521.8611450195312,
        451.6323547363281
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "the angular velocity of the last rotating mass, x27, both with and without noise. Following",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        455.49407958984375,
        521.9224853515625,
        466.2481994628906
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Althoff and Krogh (2012), we simulate trajectories with a sampling time of δt = 0.0005 over",
      "bbox": [
        89.5989990234375,
        469.0430908203125,
        522.209716796875,
        478.7303771972656
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "a horizon of 2 seconds (K = 4000), and consider their set of initial states I 6. We also define",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        480.7135925292969,
        521.9767456054688,
        492.2803649902344
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "the trajectory division setting as N = 4000, Tq = 1, q ∈[N]. The ReLU NN models are",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        495.53118896484375,
        521.974853515625,
        507.4653625488281
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "with structure [27, 54, 27]. To reduce the training runtime, we again follow the analytical",
      "bbox": [
